1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,832
Every program you run is just
numbers, flowing in and out of memory.

2
00:00:06,633 --> 00:00:10,921
Your Mac's processor can add two of
them in a fraction of a nanosecond.

3
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:13,260
But first, it has to fetch them.

4
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:15,203
And that's where the trouble starts.

5
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:20,815
Slow time down, so that one tick of
the processor's clock lasts one second.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,219
Grabbing a number from right beside
the core takes about three seconds.

7
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:27,525
Fetching it from main memory?

8
00:00:27,525 --> 00:00:28,699
About five minutes.

9
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:31,583
And from the SSD — about two days.

10
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:36,604
So how does a chip that thinks
in nanoseconds keep itself fed?

11
00:00:36,604 --> 00:00:37,516
Today: memory.

12
00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:51,272
For decades, processors got faster
much more quickly than memory did.

13
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:57,211
In 1995, two computer scientists gave
the growing gap a name: the memory wall.

14
00:00:57,533 --> 00:00:57,878
Today,

15
00:00:57,878 --> 00:01:02,708
a single core can get through hundreds of
instructions in the time it takes to fetch

16
00:01:02,708 --> 00:01:04,317
one number from main memory.

17
00:01:04,633 --> 00:01:08,751
If it had to wait every time, it would
spend almost its whole life waiting.

18
00:01:10,033 --> 00:01:12,593
So computers build memory like a pyramid.

19
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:16,676
At the top, a few dozen
registers, inside the core itself.

20
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,549
Below them, small, fast caches, made of
SRAM: six transistors for every single bit.

21
00:01:23,867 --> 00:01:28,751
On the M1, each performance core
has 128 kilobytes of data cache.

22
00:01:28,751 --> 00:01:34,085
The cores share 12 megabytes more,
and the whole chip shares another 8.

23
00:01:34,433 --> 00:01:37,258
Then main memory: gigabytes of DRAM.

24
00:01:37,258 --> 00:01:39,063
Dense, cheap, and slow.

25
00:01:39,367 --> 00:01:42,908
And then the SSD: enormous,
permanent, and slower still.

26
00:01:43,233 --> 00:01:45,772
Every step down is
bigger, and further away.

27
00:01:49,567 --> 00:01:52,724
When the core needs a number,
it checks its own cache first.

28
00:01:53,033 --> 00:01:54,935
If it isn't there, that's a miss.

29
00:01:54,935 --> 00:01:59,431
The request travels down, and comes back
with the number's whole neighborhood:

30
00:01:59,431 --> 00:02:00,180
a cache line.

31
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:05,337
On Apple silicon, macOS reports
cache lines of 128 bytes:

32
00:02:05,337 --> 00:02:08,137
room for sixteen numbers at once.

33
00:02:08,467 --> 00:02:10,443
Because programs are predictable.

34
00:02:10,443 --> 00:02:13,797
Use a number, and you'll
probably use its neighbor next.

35
00:02:13,797 --> 00:02:16,552
Use it once, and you'll
probably use it again.

36
00:02:16,867 --> 00:02:20,195
So one slow trip pays for
a whole run of fast ones.

37
00:02:20,195 --> 00:02:23,523
Caches bet on this, and
they win almost every time.

38
00:02:24,833 --> 00:02:26,093
But a cache is small.

39
00:02:26,093 --> 00:02:27,713
So where does each line go?

40
00:02:28,033 --> 00:02:31,365
Part of the address picks a
set: a short row of slots.

41
00:02:31,365 --> 00:02:35,436
Another part, the tag, records
which line is sitting in each slot.

42
00:02:35,767 --> 00:02:38,524
The cache checks every
slot in the set at once.

43
00:02:38,524 --> 00:02:39,521
A match is a hit.

44
00:02:39,833 --> 00:02:41,129
And when a set is full,

45
00:02:41,129 --> 00:02:45,465
the line that hasn't been used for the
longest is usually the one thrown out.

46
00:02:46,767 --> 00:02:49,534
The best caches don't
even wait to be asked.

47
00:02:49,534 --> 00:02:53,497
A prefetcher watches the stream
of requests, spots the pattern,

48
00:02:53,497 --> 00:02:56,516
and fetches lines before
the program needs them.

49
00:02:56,833 --> 00:02:58,195
Writes are lazy, too.

50
00:02:58,195 --> 00:03:00,917
A change stays in the cache, marked dirty,

51
00:03:00,917 --> 00:03:05,196
and only travels down to memory
when that line is finally evicted.

52
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:10,425
But with many cores, many caches
can hold copies of the same line.

53
00:03:10,733 --> 00:03:14,524
So when one core writes, every
other copy is invalidated,

54
00:03:14,524 --> 00:03:16,984
and no core ever reads a stale value.

55
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:20,998
The caches keep each other
honest, constantly, in hardware.

56
00:03:20,998 --> 00:03:22,377
It's called coherence.

57
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:28,525
Now, down into main memory itself.

58
00:03:28,833 --> 00:03:33,739
On an Apple silicon Mac, the memory chips
sit right on the processor's package,

59
00:03:33,739 --> 00:03:35,105
just millimeters away.

60
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:39,529
Inside each one: billions of cells,
laid out in grids called banks.

61
00:03:39,867 --> 00:03:44,071
And each cell is just one
transistor and one tiny capacitor:

62
00:03:44,071 --> 00:03:48,485
a bucket that's either holding
charge, a one, or empty, a zero.

63
00:03:49,767 --> 00:03:53,093
To read, the memory opens
an entire row at once.

64
00:03:53,093 --> 00:03:58,705
Every capacitor in that row spills its
charge onto a long wire, called a bitline.

65
00:03:59,033 --> 00:04:01,127
The signal is incredibly faint.

66
00:04:01,127 --> 00:04:03,895
A sense amplifier catches the tiny nudge,

67
00:04:03,895 --> 00:04:07,204
and pushes it all the way
to a clean one or zero.

68
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:12,535
Reading drains the buckets, so the amplifiers
immediately write every value back.

69
00:04:12,867 --> 00:04:14,998
The open row waits in a row buffer,

70
00:04:14,998 --> 00:04:18,285
and the requested bytes
stream out in one quick burst.

71
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:20,768
And there's a catch.

72
00:04:20,768 --> 00:04:22,053
Those capacitors leak.

73
00:04:22,367 --> 00:04:27,060
Left alone, the charge slowly drains
away, and the ones fade into zeros.

74
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:31,382
So the memory constantly reads
and rewrites itself: every row,

75
00:04:31,382 --> 00:04:33,373
at least thirty times a second.

76
00:04:33,667 --> 00:04:38,253
Your Mac's memory is forgetting everything,
all the time, and being reminded.

77
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:42,460
Pack the cells this tightly,
and something stranger happens.

78
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:46,835
Open one row over and over, and
bits in the rows beside it can flip.

79
00:04:46,835 --> 00:04:50,760
It's called Rowhammer, and
today's memory watches out for it.

80
00:04:54,533 --> 00:04:58,685
On many PCs, the graphics card
has its own separate memory.

81
00:04:58,685 --> 00:05:02,555
Data has to be copied across
before the GPU can use it.

82
00:05:02,867 --> 00:05:05,185
Apple silicon works differently.

83
00:05:05,185 --> 00:05:10,184
The CPU, the GPU, and the Neural
Engine all share one pool of memory.

84
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:11,236
No copies.

85
00:05:11,236 --> 00:05:15,577
The GPU can work on the very
same bytes the CPU just wrote.

86
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:20,301
And with the memory this close,
the connection can be wide:

87
00:05:20,301 --> 00:05:23,210
an M4 moves 120 gigabytes every second.

88
00:05:23,210 --> 00:05:25,969
An M4 Max, more than half a terabyte.

89
00:05:29,767 --> 00:05:30,798
One last trick.

90
00:05:30,798 --> 00:05:33,756
No program ever sees
a real memory address.

91
00:05:34,067 --> 00:05:36,445
Every address it uses is virtual.

92
00:05:36,445 --> 00:05:41,346
Memory is split into pages, sixteen
kilobytes each on Apple silicon,

93
00:05:41,346 --> 00:05:45,309
and a page table maps every
virtual page to a real one.

94
00:05:45,633 --> 00:05:49,246
Looking an address up means
walking down a tree of tables:

95
00:05:49,246 --> 00:05:52,609
several more trips to memory,
for every single access.

96
00:05:52,933 --> 00:05:56,900
So the chip keeps yet another
cache, just for translations.

97
00:05:56,900 --> 00:06:00,464
It's called the translation
lookaside buffer, or TLB.

98
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:03,148
And because those pages are big,

99
00:06:03,148 --> 00:06:08,945
each entry in the TLB covers four times
as much memory as it did on older Intel

100
00:06:08,945 --> 00:06:09,312
Macs.

101
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:14,344
And when a program touches a
page that isn't in memory at all,

102
00:06:14,344 --> 00:06:17,243
the hardware stops and
asks the kernel for help.

103
00:06:17,243 --> 00:06:18,451
That's a page fault.

104
00:06:18,767 --> 00:06:22,804
The kernel finds the data, maps
it in, and the program carries on,

105
00:06:22,804 --> 00:06:24,761
never knowing anything happened.

106
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:29,732
When memory runs short, macOS squeezes
pages that haven't been used lately,

107
00:06:29,732 --> 00:06:32,140
compressing them right there in memory.

108
00:06:32,433 --> 00:06:36,419
Only when that isn't enough does
it move pages out to the SSD.

109
00:06:36,419 --> 00:06:37,191
That's swap.

110
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,325
So, one more time: a single load.

111
00:06:41,833 --> 00:06:45,106
The core asks the TLB
for the real address.

112
00:06:45,106 --> 00:06:46,096
It checks L1.

113
00:06:46,096 --> 00:06:46,705
Then L2.

114
00:06:46,705 --> 00:06:48,380
Then the system cache.

115
00:06:48,380 --> 00:06:50,968
The memory controller opens a row.

116
00:06:50,968 --> 00:06:54,469
Sense amplifiers wake
thousands of capacitors.

117
00:06:54,469 --> 00:06:58,047
And a burst of bytes
races back up to the core.

118
00:06:58,367 --> 00:07:00,667
All in about a hundred nanoseconds.

119
00:07:00,667 --> 00:07:04,020
And almost every time, it
never has to go that far.

120
00:07:04,333 --> 00:07:05,552
Next time: storage.

121
00:07:05,552 --> 00:07:08,376
Where your files go
when the power goes out,

122
00:07:08,376 --> 00:07:12,547
and how billions of tiny electron
traps keep them safe for years.
